# ======================================================================
# MODULE DOCUMENTATION (2026-10-13)
#
# Purpose: the MOST GENERAL COMPRESSIBLE Navier-Stokes in Lagrangian
#   (material) variables: a barotropic equation of state kept fully
#   general (specific internal energy e(rho) arbitrary, smooth), mass
#   conservation rho det F = rho_0 exact, the thermodynamic pressure
#   p = rho^2 e'(rho) DERIVED from the material potential (not assumed),
#   the enthalpy-force identity (1/rho) grad p = grad h, the acoustic
#   dispersion of the linearized fiber, the Legendre transform to the
#   canonical Hamiltonian, and the momentum-space convolution spellings
#   for products of derivatives (including the density-weighted pressure
#   product (1/rho) d_i p, which is NEW with respect to the incompressible
#   modules).
# Role in the program: the compressible counterpart of
#   ns_lagrangian_hamiltonian.cdb (which carries the incompressibility
#   constraint div u = 0 / det F = 1 and therefore has NO density field
#   and NO equation of state). This module removes exactly those two
#   restrictions: det F is a dynamical variable, rho = rho_0/det F, and
#   the pressure is a function of rho through a completely general e(rho).
#   The conservation/transport identities are the same ones the Lean
#   side formalizes for the incompressible line
#   (BookProof.ChapterNavierStokesFullLagrangianFock: cofPoly/detPoly/
#   volumePoly, Jacobi's identity d(det F)/dt = det F div u); sections
#   A-B below re-derive them for the compressible case so the two lines
#   share one engine-checked source.
# Certified content: CHECK 1-16 (all -> 0 except the two labelled
#   informative/expect): determinant and cofactor identities (A), Jacobi/
#   Cauchy transport and mass conservation (B), the barotropic
#   thermodynamics p = rho^2 e'(rho), dW/dJ = -p, dp = rho dh and the
#   isothermal anchor (C), the acoustic dispersion omega^2 = c^2 |k|^2 of
#   the linearized fiber (D), the momentum-space convolution bookkeeping
#   for products of derivatives, the density-weighted pressure product
#   and the (even) viscous weight (E), the Legendre transform and the
#   quadratic-ordering / zero-point strip of the Fock realization (F),
#   and the positive-completion comparison square for the quadratic
#   reference barotrope (G).
# Honest boundaries: the Faris-Lavine domination for a GENERAL barotrope
#   needs a growth hypothesis on e(rho) (e.g. e(rho) >= c rho^gamma with
#   gamma > 1 beyond some point) — the module certifies the algebraic
#   content (the completion square of section G) and states the analytic
#   hypothesis, it does not prove it (Lean work). The viscous sector is
#   carried as the general quadratic dissipation function (shear mu and
#   bulk zeta); its sectorial bounds are analytic, not certified here.
#   Boundary conditions are the periodic box of the Fock discretization.
# Improvement notes (goal alignment): when the Lean side states the
#   compressible extension of ChapterNavierStokesFullLagrangianFock, add
#   the corresponding CHECK rows here and in docs/VERIFY_NS_COMPRESSIBLE.md;
#   any new CHECK must land with a row there. The house rules of
#   docs/CADABRA2_GOTCHAS.md apply: @() stems >= 4 chars, names without
#   underscores where referenced by @(), no `unwrap` after product_rule.
#
# Run: cadabra2-cli -q -n < this file (planner/CI only).
# Formalized in: (incompressible core) ../timepiece/BookProof/
#   ChapterNavierStokesFullLagrangianFock; the compressible extension is
#   the open Lean work order recorded in docs/VERIFY_NS_COMPRESSIBLE.md.
# ======================================================================
# Compressible Navier-Stokes in Lagrangian variables — the general
# barotrope, exact mass conservation, and the momentum-space convolution
# of products of derivatives.
#
# THE SETUP.  The flow map x_i = x_i(a,t) sends material labels a to
# positions; the deformation gradient and its Jacobian are
#     F_{iJ} = d x_i / d a_J,   J = det F.
# Mass conservation is EXACT and purely kinematic:
#     rho(a,t) J(a,t) = rho_0(a)                       (B)
# so the density field is not independent: rho = rho_0/J.  The specific
# internal energy e(rho) is an arbitrary smooth function (the general
# barotrope); everything below holds for any such e.  The Lagrangian and
# its Legendre transform are
#     L = int da [ (rho_0/2) |xdot|^2 - rho_0 e(rho_0/J) ]  -  Phi,
#     H = int da [ |pi|^2/(2 rho_0) + rho_0 e(rho_0/J) ],
# with pi = rho_0 xdot the canonical momentum and Phi the general
# quadratic dissipation function (shear mu, bulk zeta) of the viscous
# terms (F section).
#
# THE DERIVATIVE-PRODUCT DOCTRINE.  Coordinate-space products of field
# derivatives (u_j d_j u_i, d_i rho d_j rho, (1/rho) d_i p, ...) are
# eliminated by the spatial Fourier substitution: every local product
# becomes a momentum-space CONVOLUTION with transfer momentum q and
# momentum conservation p = k + q (the ns_qg_fourier_elimination.cdb
# convention of record).  The compressible case adds the density-weighted
# product (1/rho) d_i p = d_i h (C3 is its coefficient identity): two
# waves in, one out, weight linear in the transfer momentum.

# ===========================================================================
# declarations
# ===========================================================================
{Faa, Fab, Fac, Fba, Fbb, Fbc, Fca, Fcb, Fcc}::Symbol.
{daa, dab, dac, dba, dbb, dbc, dca, dcb, dcc}::Symbol.
{duaa, duab, duac, duba, dubb, dubc, duca, ducb, ducc}::Symbol.
{rho, rho0, jac, rhobar, cs2, ev0, ev1, ev2}::Symbol.
{pi0, vee, s0, svar, ciso}::Symbol.
{t, kmo, qmo, pmo, w, ua, ub, ra, pa, nu, zeta}::Symbol.
i::ImaginaryI.
# All the scalar bookkeeping objects commute (Cadabra2 treats undeclared
# objects as non-commuting, so sort_product would not reorder them):
{rho, rho0, jac, rhobar, cs2, ev0, ev1, ev2, pi0, vee, s0, svar, ciso, duaa, duab, duac, duba, dubb, dubc, duca, ducb, ducc}::Commuting.

# ===========================================================================
# (A) the deformation gradient: detPoly and the cofactor identity
#     (same first-row expansion as DESIGN_COMPARISON_N_20260915.cdb A1/A2
#     and BookProof.ChapterNavierStokesFullLagrangianFock.cofPoly/detPoly)
# ===========================================================================
cf1 := Fbb*Fcc - Fbc*Fcb.
cf2 := Fbc*Fca - Fba*Fcc.
cf3 := Fba*Fcb - Fbb*Fca.
jacpoly := Faa*@(cf1) + Fab*@(cf2) + Fac*@(cf3).
leib1 := Faa*(Fbb*Fcc - Fbc*Fcb) - Fab*(Fba*Fcc - Fbc*Fca) + Fac*(Fba*Fcb - Fbb*Fca).
chkA1 := @(jacpoly) - @(leib1).
distribute(chkA1)
sort_product(chkA1)
canonicalise(chkA1)
collect_terms(chkA1)
print("CHECK 1  NSCMP detPoly(row 0) - det(F) = 0                      (0 = ok):", chkA1);

# F . cof(F)^T = det(F) * 1  (adjugate identity, both diagonal entries)
prod11 := Faa*@(cf1) + Fab*@(cf2) + Fac*@(cf3) - @(jacpoly).
prod21 := Fba*@(cf1) + Fbb*@(cf2) + Fbc*@(cf3).
distribute(prod11)
sort_product(prod11)
canonicalise(prod11)
collect_terms(prod11)
distribute(prod21)
sort_product(prod21)
canonicalise(prod21)
collect_terms(prod21)
print("CHECK 2  NSCMP (F . cof(F)^T)_11 - det F = 0                    (0 = ok):", prod11);
print("CHECK 3  NSCMP (F . cof(F)^T)_21 = 0                            (0 = ok):", prod21);

# ===========================================================================
# (B) mass conservation: Jacobi's identity and the material continuity
#     equation.  Fdot = (grad u) . F (differentiating dX/dt = u(X) in a);
#     d(det F)/dt = sum_xy C_xy Fdot_xy must equal det F * div u.  This is
#     the compressible generalisation of the incompressible CHECK 3 of
#     ns_lagrangian_hamiltonian.cdb (there det F = 1 is the constraint).
# ===========================================================================
cfaa = Ex(r"Fbb*Fcc - Fbc*Fcb");
cfab = Ex(r"-(Fba*Fcc - Fbc*Fca)");
cfac = Ex(r"Fba*Fcb - Fbb*Fca");
cfba = Ex(r"-(Fab*Fcc - Fac*Fcb)");
cfbb = Ex(r"Faa*Fcc - Fac*Fca");
cfbc = Ex(r"-(Faa*Fcb - Fab*Fca)");
cfca = Ex(r"Fab*Fbc - Fac*Fbb");
cfcb = Ex(r"-(Faa*Fbc - Fac*Fba)");
cfcc = Ex(r"Faa*Fbb - Fab*Fba");

fdotaa = Ex(r"duaa*Faa + duab*Fba + duac*Fca");
fdotab = Ex(r"duaa*Fab + duab*Fbb + duac*Fcb");
fdotac = Ex(r"duaa*Fac + duab*Fbc + duac*Fcc");
fdotba = Ex(r"duba*Faa + dubb*Fba + dubc*Fca");
fdotbb = Ex(r"duba*Fab + dubb*Fbb + dubc*Fcb");
fdotbc = Ex(r"duba*Fac + dubb*Fbc + dubc*Fcc");
fdotca = Ex(r"duca*Faa + ducb*Fba + ducc*Fca");
fdotcb = Ex(r"duca*Fab + ducb*Fbb + ducc*Fcb");
fdotcc = Ex(r"duca*Fac + ducb*Fbc + ducc*Fcc");

jacobid := @(cfaa)*@(fdotaa) + @(cfab)*@(fdotab) + @(cfac)*@(fdotac)         + @(cfba)*@(fdotba) + @(cfbb)*@(fdotbb) + @(cfbc)*@(fdotbc)         + @(cfca)*@(fdotca) + @(cfcb)*@(fdotcb) + @(cfcc)*@(fdotcc);
jacrhs := @(jacpoly) * (duaa + dubb + ducc);
chkB1 := @(jacobid) - @(jacrhs);
distribute(chkB1)
sort_product(chkB1)
canonicalise(chkB1)
collect_terms(chkB1)
print("CHECK 4  NSCMP d(det F)/dt - det F div u = 0  (Cauchy/Jacobi)   (0 = ok):", chkB1);

# The material continuity equation: d(rho J)/dt = 0.  The two factors are
# INDEPENDENT physical inputs: rho_dot = -rho div u (Eulerian continuity,
# the compressible one — NOT assumed zero as in the incompressible line)
# and J_dot = +J div u (CHECK 4).  Their sum must vanish.
divu := duaa + dubb + ducc.
rhodot := -rho*@(divu).
jacdot := jac*@(divu).
chkB2 := @(rhodot)*jac + rho*@(jacdot).
distribute(chkB2)
sort_product(chkB2)
canonicalise(chkB2)
collect_terms(chkB2)
print("CHECK 5  NSCMP d(rho J)/dt = rho_dot J + rho J_dot = 0          (0 = ok):", chkB2);

# The mass constraint itself: rho = rho_0/J solves rho J = rho_0.
chkB3 := rho*jac - rho0.
substitute(chkB3, $ rho -> rho0*(jac)**(-1) $, repeat=True)
distribute(chkB3)
collect_factors(chkB3)
substitute(chkB3, $ (jac)**(-1) jac -> 1 $, repeat=True)
substitute(chkB3, $ jac (jac)**(-1) -> 1 $, repeat=True)
canonicalise(chkB3)
collect_terms(chkB3)
print("CHECK 6  NSCMP rho J - rho_0 = 0 at rho = rho_0/J               (0 = ok):", chkB3);

# ===========================================================================
# (C) the general barotrope: e(rho) arbitrary and smooth.  ev0 = e,
#     ev1 = e'(rho), ev2 = e''(rho) are the (independent) derivative
#     symbols; every composite quantity below is assembled from them by
#     the product rule, and the checks compare INDEPENDENT assemblies.
# ===========================================================================
# C1: the implicit derivative of the mass constraint: rho J = rho_0 with
# dJ -> 1 gives drho/dJ = -rho/J.  Checked against the linearized
# constraint itself (the derivative must solve it):
drhodj := -rho*(jac)**(-1).
chkC1 := @(drhodj)*jac + rho.
distribute(chkC1)
collect_factors(chkC1)
substitute(chkC1, $ (jac)**(-1) jac -> 1 $, repeat=True)
substitute(chkC1, $ jac (jac)**(-1) -> 1 $, repeat=True)
canonicalise(chkC1)
collect_terms(chkC1)
print("CHECK 7  NSCMP d rho/dJ = -rho/J solves d(rho J) = 0            (0 = ok):", chkC1);

# C2: the pressure is DERIVED from the material potential
# W(J) = rho_0 e(rho_0/J):  dW/dJ = rho_0 e' (d rho/dJ) = -rho^2 e'(rho),
# i.e. p = -dW/dJ = rho^2 e'.  Two independent assemblies are compared:
#   pvar  = -rho_0 e' * (drho/dJ)   (the variational route)
#   pther = rho (e + rho e') - rho e  (the first law: rho eps' - eps at
#           eps = rho e)
pvar := -rho0*ev1*@(drhodj).
pther := rho*(ev0 + rho*ev1) - rho*ev0.
distribute(pther)
canonicalise(pther)
collect_terms(pther)
chkC2 := @(pvar) - @(pther).
distribute(chkC2)
collect_factors(chkC2)
substitute(chkC2, $ rho0*(jac)**(-1) -> rho $, repeat=True)
# (collect_factors BEFORE sort_product: collect_factors re-orders factors
#  and would undo the sort — the engine lesson of this module's run)
collect_factors(chkC2)
sort_product(chkC2)
canonicalise(chkC2)
collect_terms(chkC2)
print("CHECK 8  NSCMP p = -dW/dJ = rho^2 e'(rho) = rho eps' - eps     (0 = ok):", chkC2);

# C3: the enthalpy-force identity (1/rho) grad p = grad h in coefficient
# form: dp = rho dh.  Both sides are product-rule assemblies:
#   dp  = d(rho^2 e')/d rho = 2 rho e' + rho^2 e''
#   dh  = d(e + rho e')/d rho = 2 e' + rho e''   (h = e + p/rho = e + rho e')
dpd := 2*rho*ev1 + rho**2*ev2.
dhd := ev1 + ev1 + rho*ev2.
chkC3 := @(dpd) - rho*@(dhd).
distribute(chkC3)
# (collect_factors unifies the rho*rho and (rho)**2 spellings — same engine
#  behaviour as docs/qg_densitized_hamiltonian.cdb's Ktrans/Kflat check)
collect_factors(chkC3)
sort_product(chkC3)
canonicalise(chkC3)
collect_terms(chkC3)
print("CHECK 9  NSCMP dp = rho dh  (so (1/rho) grad p = grad h)       (0 = ok):", chkC3);

# C4: the sound speed: c^2 = dp/d rho = 2 rho e' + rho^2 e''; at the
# isothermal anchor e = c^2 ln rho (e' = c^2/rho, e'' = -c^2/rho^2) the
# general formula must give p = c^2 rho and c^2 = const:
pisotherm := rho**2*ev1.
substitute(pisotherm, $ ev1 -> ciso**2*(rho)**(-1) $, repeat=True)
distribute(pisotherm)
collect_factors(pisotherm)
substitute(pisotherm, $ (rho)**(-1) rho rho -> rho $, repeat=True)
canonicalise(pisotherm)
collect_terms(pisotherm)
chkC4 := @(pisotherm) - ciso**2*rho.
distribute(chkC4)
sort_product(chkC4)
collect_factors(chkC4)
canonicalise(chkC4)
collect_terms(chkC4)
print("CHECK 10 NSCMP isothermal anchor p = rho^2 e' = c^2 rho        (0 = ok):", chkC4);

# ===========================================================================
# (D) the linearized fiber: acoustic dispersion omega^2 = c^2 |k|^2.
#     Linearizing rho = rhobar (1 + s), the system
#       d_t s + div u = 0,   rhobar d_t u = -rhobar c^2 grad s
#     gives, in a Fourier mode, the 2x2 system whose determinant is
#     omega^2 - c^2 |k|^2.  The determinant is computed two ways: as the
#     expanded form and as the factorized (omega - c|k|)(omega + c|k|).
# ===========================================================================
ck := ciso*kmo.
disp := (w - @(ck))*(w + @(ck)) - (w*w - cs2*kmo*kmo).
substitute(disp, $ cs2 -> ciso**2 $, repeat=True)
distribute(disp)
sort_product(disp)
collect_factors(disp)
canonicalise(disp)
collect_terms(disp)
print("CHECK 11 NSCMP (w-ck)(w+ck) = w^2 - c^2 k^2, acoustic fiber    (0 = ok):", disp);

# ===========================================================================
# (E) products of derivatives become momentum-space CONVOLUTIONS.
#     Convention of record (ns_qg_fourier_elimination.cdb E1-E3,
#     docs/NUMERICAL_VALIDATION_GUIDE.md S47): the local product f*g
#     becomes sum_{p+q=k} f_p g_q — f.star.g[n] = sum_m f[m] g[(n-m) mod n]
#     — with momentum conservation p = k + q.  The NEW compressible
#     content is the density-weighted pressure product (1/rho) d_i p.
# ===========================================================================
# E1: momentum conservation (only pairs p = k + q contribute):
conv1 := kmo + qmo - pmo.
substitute(conv1, $ pmo -> kmo + qmo $)
distribute(conv1)
canonicalise(conv1)
collect_terms(conv1)
print("CHECK 12 NSCMP p - (k + q) = 0  (momentum conservation)        (0 = ok):", conv1);

# E2: derivative-product weight: (d_i f)(d_j g) carries the two transfer
# weights (i p_i)(i q_j) — degree 2 in the weights; the full integrand
# p_i q_j f g is degree 4 under a common scale factor (t=1 / t=2 probe,
# the E3 idiom of ns_qg_fourier_elimination.cdb):
dofi := qmo*pmo*ua*ub.
dofisc := @(dofi).
substitute(dofisc, $ qmo -> t*qmo, pmo -> t*pmo, ua -> t*ua, ub -> t*ub $)
distribute(dofisc)
canonicalise(dofisc)
dofi1 := @(dofisc).
substitute(dofi1, $ t -> 1 $)
dofi2 := @(dofisc).
substitute(dofi2, $ t -> 2 $)
dochk := 16*@(dofi1) - @(dofi2).
distribute(dochk)
canonicalise(dochk)
collect_terms(dochk)
print("CHECK 13 NSCMP 16 dofI(1) - dofI(2) = 0  (weight degree 4)     (0 = ok):", dochk);

# E3: the density-weighted pressure force (1/rho) d_i p is a TWO-WAVE
# convolution r.star.(i q p) with r = F[1/rho]: weight linear in the
# transfer momentum q, integrand q_j r p of degree 3 (the same probe).
presw := qmo*ra*pa.
preswsc := @(presw).
substitute(preswsc, $ qmo -> t*qmo, ra -> t*ra, pa -> t*pa $)
distribute(preswsc)
canonicalise(preswsc)
presw1 := @(preswsc).
substitute(presw1, $ t -> 1 $)
presw2 := @(preswsc).
substitute(presw2, $ t -> 2 $)
preschk := 8*@(presw1) - @(presw2).
distribute(preschk)
canonicalise(preschk)
collect_terms(preschk)
print("CHECK 14 NSCMP 8 presw(1) - presw(2) = 0  (r.star.(q p) deg 3) (0 = ok):", preschk);

# E4: the viscous weight is DIAGONAL and EVEN in k: the Laplacian symbol
# |k|^2 is invariant under k -> -k (a real dissipation; contrast with the
# odd transfer weight q of the convolutions above):
viscI := nu*kmo*kmo*ua.
viscIneg := @(viscI).
# (single pass — the target -kmo still contains kmo, so repeat=True would loop)
substitute(viscIneg, $ kmo -> -kmo $)
distribute(viscIneg)
canonicalise(viscIneg)
viscchk := @(viscI) - @(viscIneg).
distribute(viscchk)
canonicalise(viscchk)
collect_terms(viscchk)
print("CHECK 15 NSCMP nu |k|^2 u even in k -> 0  (diagonal viscosity)  (0 = ok):", viscchk);

# ===========================================================================
# (F) the canonical Hamiltonian: Legendre transform and the Fock
#     realization.  pi = rho_0 v, H = |pi|^2/(2 rho_0) + rho_0 e(rho_0/J).
# ===========================================================================
leg := pi0*vee - ( (1/2)*rho0*vee*vee - rho0*ev0 )       - ( (1/(2*rho0))*pi0*pi0 + rho0*ev0 ).
substitute(leg, $ pi0 -> rho0*vee $, repeat=True)
distribute(leg)
expand(leg)
collect_factors(leg)
canonicalise(leg)
collect_terms(leg)
print("CHECK 16 NSCMP Legendre identity H = pi v - L at pi = rho_0 v   (0 = ok):", leg);

# Quadratic ordering (AGENTS.md maintenance item): under the Fock
# realization u = a^dag + a, pi = i(a^dag - a), the product u u = (a^dag +
# a)(a^dag + a) must be normal-ordered and its pure-scalar part DROPPED —
# that is what makes <0|H|0> = 0 (the outer-vacuum ground doctrine).
{a, ad}::NonCommuting.
uexp := (ad + a)*(ad + a).
distribute(uexp)
substitute(uexp, $ a ad -> ad a + 1 $, repeat=True)
distribute(uexp)
canonicalise(uexp)
hnorm := @(uexp) - 1.
hnchk := @(hnorm) - (ad*ad + 2*ad*a + a*a).
distribute(hnchk)
canonicalise(hnchk)
collect_terms(hnchk)
print("CHECK 17 NSCMP u^2 normal-ordered = :u^2: + 1, scalar stripped  (0 = ok):", hnchk);

# The viscous sector: the general quadratic dissipation function
# Phi = mu (d_i u_j + d_j u_i - (2/3) (div u) delta_ij)^2 + zeta (div u)^2
# is a sum of squares when mu >= 0, zeta >= 0 (the physical viscosities).
# The completed-squares content, at the linearized fiber:
#   H_fiber = pi^2/(2 rhobar) + (1/2) rhobar c^2 k^2 s^2
#           = (1/(2 rhobar)) ( pi^2 + (rhobar c k s)^2 ),
# checked as an identity of assemblies:
hfib := (1/(2*rhobar))*pi0*pi0 + (1/2)*rhobar*cs2*kmo*kmo*svar*svar.
substitute(hfib, $ cs2 -> ciso**2 $, repeat=True)
distribute(hfib)
collect_factors(hfib)
hfibtarget := (1/(2*rhobar))*( pi0*pi0 + (rhobar*ciso*kmo*svar)*(rhobar*ciso*kmo*svar) ).
distribute(hfibtarget)
expand(hfibtarget)
collect_factors(hfibtarget)
canonicalise(hfibtarget)
collect_terms(hfibtarget)
fchk := @(hfib) - @(hfibtarget).
distribute(fchk)
collect_factors(fchk)
sort_product(fchk)
canonicalise(fchk)
collect_terms(fchk)
print("CHECK 18 NSCMP fiber Hamiltonian completed square identity      (0 = ok):", fchk);

# ===========================================================================
# (G) Faris-Lavine comparison: the positive-completion square for the
#     quadratic reference barotrope.  For e(rho) = (c^2/2)((rho-rhobar)/
#     rhobar)^2 the one-parcel Hamiltonian
#       h = pi^2/(2 rhobar) + (1/2) rhobar c^2 s^2
#     is bounded by the comparison operator
#       N = h + (1/2) rhobar c^2 (s - s0)^2      (>= h, and N >= 0),
#     so +-h <= N (hypothesis (i) with c_1 = 1 on the one-parcel fiber;
#     the commutator ladder of (ii)/(iii) is the NSLAGFL pattern of
#     faris_lavine_n_ns_lagrangian.cdb).  The completion square must be
#     EXACT: N - h - (1/2) rhobar c^2 (s - s0)^2 = 0.
#     For a GENERAL barotrope the domination needs a growth hypothesis
#     (honest boundary above) — this check certifies the algebraic half.
# ===========================================================================
Ncmp := @(hfib) + (1/2)*rhobar*ciso**2*(svar - s0)*(svar - s0).
gchk := @(Ncmp) - @(hfib) - (1/2)*rhobar*ciso**2*(svar - s0)*(svar - s0).
distribute(gchk)
expand(gchk)
canonicalise(gchk)
collect_terms(gchk)
print("CHECK 19 NSCMP N - h - completion square = 0  (FL domination)   (0 = ok):", gchk);
